ally if identical rows are considered same, then selecting both would be same set? But we need each element appears exactly once. If we choose both identical rows, they'd duplicate elements. But if they are identical, they'd duplicate coverage. So they cannot both be chosen anyway because they'd duplicate elements. So the only difference is that we don't count multiple ways that differ only by swapping identical rows. So we need to count distinct subsets of row indices modulo permutations of identical rows. Equivalent to counting subsets of rows where we treat identical rows as indistinguishable. So we can just count subsets of unique row contents. But we must ensure that we don't count duplicates due to identical rows. So we can treat each unique row content as a single representative. So the count is number of subsets of unique rows that form exact cover. So we can just deduplicate rows.\n\nThus algorithm: read packet: get n, rows (list of lists of ints?), labeled flag. Compute unique rows if labeled=false. Then count exact covers. Use enumeration if number of rows small. But we don't know. Could use inclusion-exclusion? But maybe small.\n\nWe can use quotient tool: groups of identical rows? But quotient expects groups of rows with identical content. We can use quotient to compute number of covers by grouping identical rows"}}],"created":1790940726,"model":"C:\\Users\\d2j3\\Documents\\Codex\\2026-09-10\\napraw\\outputs\\moon-native\\models\\gpt-oss-20b-MXFP4.gguf","system_fingerprint":"b10809-5266f24da","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":768,"prompt_tokens":3643,"total_tokens":4411,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-cznaeFMFOa23JpYDqbcwZGyLhBHJUmCT","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":3643,"prompt_ms":426.36,"prompt_per_token_ms":0.11703541037606369,"prompt_per_second":8544.42255371048,"predicted_n":768,"predicted_ms":2514.379,"predicted_per_token_ms":3.278199478487614,"predicted_per_second":305.0455003004718}}